  "content": "<p><strong>Unit 9 - Inequalities — Title &amp; Goal</strong><br />Comprehensive lesson covering Unit 9 - Inequalities for SAT Math preparation.</p>\n\n\n<h3>Key idea</h3>\n<p>Read the idea and scan the rules. Focus on how the symbols behave.</p>\n<p>Rules are similar to solving equations, with <strong>one key difference</strong>:<br/>\n- When multiplying or dividing both sides by a <strong>negative number</strong>, the inequality sign <strong>flips</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 1</strong><br/>\nSolve: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>âˆ’</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></math> </p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>18</mn><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo>â‡’</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>\n<p><strong>Example 2</strong><br/>\nSolve: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>âˆ’</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>â‰¥</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math> </p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>âˆ’</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>â‰¥</mo><mn>4</mn><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo>â‡’</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>â‰¤</mo><mo>âˆ’</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>\n<hr/>\n<p>Translate carefully from words to math.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 3</strong><br/>\nA concert ticket costs $40. You have at most $250. How many tickets can you buy?</p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>40</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>â‰¤</mo><mn>250</mn><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo>â‡’</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>â‰¤</mo><mn>6.25</mn></mrow></math>\n<p>So maximum whole tickets = 6.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 4</strong><br/>\nA test requires at least 70 points to pass. You currently have 42 points out of 60. How many of the remaining 40 points must you get?</p>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> = points you score on last part.  </p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>42</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>â‰¥</mo><mn>70</mn><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo>â‡’</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>â‰¥</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow></math>\n<p>So you must earn at least 28 points.</p>\n<hr/>\n<ul>\n<li>For one-variable inequalities:  </li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> → open circle at 3, shade left.  </li>\n<li>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>â‰¥</mo><mo>âˆ’</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> → closed circle at -2, shade right.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>For two-variable inequalities:  </p>\n</li>\n<li>Example: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>â‰¤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> → graph line <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Shade <strong>below</strong> because of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>â‰¤</mo></mrow></math>.  </li>\n<li>Use dashed line for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo></mrow></math> or <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo></mrow></math>; solid line for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>â‰¤</mo></mrow></math> or <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>â‰¥</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example 5</strong><br/>\nGraph solution set of:</p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mo>âˆ’</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>\n<p>Line: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>âˆ’</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, slope <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>âˆ’</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, intercept <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Dashed line. Shade region <strong>above</strong>.</p>\n<hr/>\n<p>The solution is the <strong>overlap region</strong> of all shaded areas.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 6</strong><br/>\nGraph and solve:</p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>â‰¥</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mo>âˆ’</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>\n<p>The solution region is the intersection of the two shaded half-planes.</p>\n<hr/>\n<ul>\n<li>Always flip the inequality when multiplying/dividing by negative.  </li>\n<li>Check <strong>boundary points</strong> (does equality hold?).  </li>\n<li>For word problems, highlight key phrases like “at most,” “at least,” “no more than.”  </li>\n<li>Graph shading is common on SAT - test with a sample point like <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr/>\n<p>1) Solve: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow></math><br/>\n2) Solve: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>âˆ’</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>â‰¤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math><br/>\n3) A taxi charges \\<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>\\</mi></mrow></math>2 per mile. You have at most $30. How many miles can you ride?<br/>\n4) Solve and graph: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>â‰¤</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math><br/>\n5) System of inequalities:  </p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>âˆ’</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>y</mi><mo>â‰¤</mo><mo>âˆ’</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>\n<hr/>\n<p>1) <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>17</mn><mo>â‡’</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>â‡’</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>2) <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>âˆ’</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>â‰¤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>â‡’</mo><mo>âˆ’</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>â‰¤</mo><mo>âˆ’</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>â‡’</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>â‰¥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>3) Cost equation: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>â‰¤</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>â‡’</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>â‰¤</mo><mn>26</mn><mo>â‡’</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>â‰¤</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></math>. So up to 13 miles.  </p>\n<p>4) Line: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, slope <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, intercept 3. Solid line (since <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>â‰¤</mo></mrow></math>). Shade below.  </p>\n<p>5) Graph both: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>âˆ’</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (above dashed line), <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>â‰¤</mo><mo>âˆ’</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> (below solid line). Solution = overlap region.</p>\n\n<h3>Quick practice</h3>\n<p>Try these on your own. Check your steps, not just the final answer.</p>\n1) <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>17</mn><mo>â‡’</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>â‡’</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.  \n\n<h3>Mixed practice set</h3>\n<p>These mix everything above. Time yourself and reflect on any mistakes.</p>\n",
